PYTHON BASICS

1. Data types
- Variable - stores data
- String - text
- Integer - whole numbers
- Float - decimal numbers, for example prices or distances
- Boolean - only True or False

2. Functions and operators
- len() - returns the length of text or a list
- print() - shows output on the screen
- input() - gets data from the user
- str() - converts something to text
- int() - converts something to a whole number
- float() - converts something to a decimal number
- bool() - converts something to True or False
- dir() - shows available methods and attributes
- help() - shows information about functions, commands, or modules

3. Conditions
- if - runs code when a condition is True
- elif - checks another condition
- else - runs code when nothing above is True

Logical operators:
- or - at least one condition must be True
- and - both conditions must be True
- not - changes True to False and False to True

4. Strings
- find() - finds the first position of text inside a string
- rfind() - finds the last position of text inside a string
- capitalize() - makes the first letter uppercase
- upper() - makes all letters uppercase
- lower() - makes all letters lowercase
- isdigit() - returns True if all characters are digits
- isalpha() - returns True if all characters are letters
- count() - counts how many times something appears in a string
- replace() - changes one part of text into another

5. Loops
- while - repeats code while a condition is True

6. Useful extra
- f-string - lets you put variables inside text
- result - a variable name often used for storing an answer
PYTHON COLLECTION METHODS

1. List methods
- append() - adds an element to the end of the list
- remove() - removes a specific element
- sort() - sorts items in alphabetical or numerical order
- reverse() - reverses the order of items
- clear() - removes all items from the list

2. Set methods
- add() - adds an item to the set
- clear() - removes all items from the set
- copy() - makes a copy of the set
- difference() - returns items that are in one set but not the other
- difference_update() - removes items found in another set
- discard() - removes an item if it exists
- intersection() - returns common items from two sets
- intersection_update() - updates the set with common items only
- isdisjoint() - returns True if two sets have no items in common
- issubset() - returns True if all items are in another set
- issuperset() - returns True if the set contains all items from another set
- pop() - removes and returns a random item
- remove() - removes a specific item
- symmetric_difference() - returns items that are in one set or the other, but not both
- symmetric_difference_update() - updates the set with unique items from both sets
- union() - combines items from two sets
- update() - adds items from another set

3. Dictionary methods
- copy() - makes a copy of the dictionary
- fromkeys() - creates a new dictionary with specified keys
- get() - returns the value of a key
- items() - returns key-value pairs
- keys() - returns all keys
- pop() - removes a key and returns its value
- popitem() - removes the last inserted item
- setdefault() - returns the value of a key, or adds it if missing
- update() - adds or changes items from another dictionary
- values() - returns all values

4. Useful commands
- dir() - shows all available methods and attributes
- help() - shows descriptions of methods and functions





PYTHON CHEAT SHEET

1. VARIABLES AND DATA TYPES
- Variable - stores data
- String (str) - text
- Integer (int) - whole numbers
- Float (float) - decimal numbers
- Boolean (bool) - True or False
- None - means no value

Examples:
name = "Igor"
age = 20
price = 9.99
is_active = True

2. BASIC FUNCTIONS
- print() - shows output on the screen
- input() - gets data from the user
- len() - returns the length of text, list, etc.
- type() - shows the type of a value
- str() - converts to text
- int() - converts to whole number
- float() - converts to decimal number
- bool() - converts to True or False
- dir() - shows available methods and attributes
- help() - shows information about functions and modules

3. STRING BASICS
- String - text inside quotes

Examples:
text = "hello"

Useful string methods:
- capitalize() - makes the first letter uppercase
- upper() - makes all letters uppercase
- lower() - makes all letters lowercase
- find() - finds the first position of text
- rfind() - finds the last position of text
- count() - counts how many times something appears
- replace() - replaces one part of text with another
- isdigit() - True if all characters are digits
- isalpha() - True if all characters are letters
- split() - splits text into a list
- strip() - removes spaces from the start and end

4. F-STRINGS
- f-string - lets you put variables inside text

Example:
name = "Igor"
age = 20
print(f"My name is {name} and I am {age} years old")

5. OPERATORS
Math operators:
- + addition
- - subtraction
- * multiplication
- / division
- // floor division
- % modulo, gives the remainder
- ** power

Comparison operators:
- == equal
- != not equal
- > greater than
- < less than
- >= greater than or equal
- <= less than or equal

Logical operators:
- or - at least one condition must be True
- and - both conditions must be True
- not - reverses True and False

6. CONDITIONS
- if - runs code when a condition is True
- elif - checks another condition
- else - runs code when nothing above is True

Example:
if age >= 18:
    print("Adult")
elif age >= 13:
    print("Teen")
else:
    print("Child")

7. LOOPS
while loop:
- while - repeats code while a condition is True

Example:
i = 0
while i < 5:
    print(i)
    i += 1

for loop:
- for - repeats over items in a sequence

Example:
for letter in "hello":
    print(letter)

Useful loop commands:
- break - stops the loop
- continue - skips the current round and goes to the next one
- pass - does nothing

8. LISTS
- List - ordered collection that can change

Example:
fruits = ["apple", "banana", "orange"]

List methods:
- append() - adds an element to the end of the list
- remove() - removes a specific element
- pop() - removes and returns an item
- insert() - adds an item at a chosen position
- sort() - sorts items in alphabetical or numerical order
- reverse() - reverses the order of items
- clear() - removes all items from the list
- copy() - makes a copy of the list
- index() - returns the position of an item
- count() - counts how many times an item appears

9. TUPLES
- Tuple - ordered collection that cannot be changed

Example:
fruits = ("apple", "banana", "orange")

Useful tuple idea:
- tuples are like lists, but immutable

10. SETS
- Set - unordered collection of unique items

Example:
fruits = {"apple", "banana", "orange"}

Set methods:
- add() - adds an item to the set
- clear() - removes all items from the set
- copy() - makes a copy of the set
- difference() - returns items that are in one set but not the other
- difference_update() - removes items found in another set
- discard() - removes an item if it exists
- intersection() - returns common items from two sets
- intersection_update() - updates the set with common items only
- isdisjoint() - True if two sets have no items in common
- issubset() - True if all items are in another set
- issuperset() - True if the set contains all items from another set
- pop() - removes and returns a random item
- remove() - removes a specific item
- symmetric_difference() - returns items in one set or the other, but not both
- symmetric_difference_update() - updates the set with unique items from both sets
- union() - combines items from two sets
- update() - adds items from another set

11. DICTIONARIES
- Dictionary - stores data in key-value pairs

Example:
person = {
    "name": "Igor",
    "age": 20
}

Dictionary methods:
- copy() - makes a copy of the dictionary
- fromkeys() - creates a new dictionary with specified keys
- get() - returns the value of a key
- items() - returns key-value pairs
- keys() - returns all keys
- pop() - removes a key and returns its value
- popitem() - removes the last inserted item
- setdefault() - returns the value of a key, or adds it if missing
- update() - adds or changes items from another dictionary
- values() - returns all values

12. FUNCTIONS
- function - a reusable block of code

Example:
def greet():
    print("Hello")

Useful function ideas:
- def - creates a function
- return - sends a value back from a function
- arguments - values passed into a function
- parameters - variables inside the function

Example:
def add(a, b):
    return a + b

13. MODULES
- module - a file with Python code you can reuse
- import - brings in a module

Examples:
import math
from math import sqrt

Useful idea:
- modules give you extra functions

14. ERRORS AND EXCEPTIONS
- syntax error - writing code with wrong grammar
- exception - an error that happens while the program runs

Example:
try:
    number = int(input())
except:
    print("Invalid input")

Useful words:
- try - test code
- except - handles errors
- finally - runs no matter what

15. FILES
- open() - opens a file

Example:
file = open("test.txt", "r")

Modes:
- "r" - read
- "w" - write
- "a" - append
- "x" - create
- "b" - binary
- "t" - text

Useful methods:
- read() - reads the whole file
- readline() - reads one line
- readlines() - reads all lines into a list
- write() - writes text to a file
- close() - closes the file

Better way:
with open("test.txt", "r") as file:
    print(file.read())

16. CLASSES / OOP
- class - a blueprint for creating objects
- object - an instance of a class

Example:
class Car:
    def __init__(self, brand):
        self.brand = brand

    def drive(self):
        print("Driving")

Useful words:
- self - refers to the current object
- __init__() - runs when an object is created
- method - a function inside a class
- attribute - data stored in an object

17. USEFUL BUILT-IN IDEAS
- len() - length
- range() - creates a sequence of numbers
- sum() - adds numbers
- max() - biggest value
- min() - smallest value
- sorted() - returns a sorted version
- enumerate() - gives index and value
- zip() - pairs values together

Examples:
for i in range(5):
    print(i)

18. COMMON DEBUGGING TOOLS
- print() - use it to see what your code is doing
- type() - check the type of a value
- dir() - see methods and attributes
- help() - check how something works

19. IMPORTANT PYTHON IDEAS
- indentation matters
- Python uses spaces to show blocks of code
- names should be clear and readable
- variables can change if you reassign them
- comments start with #

Example:
# this is a comment

20. QUICK REMINDERS
- True and False must start with capital letters
- strings use quotes: "text" or 'text'
- lists use []
- tuples use ()
- sets use {}
- dictionaries use {key: value}
